    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2027 Hyundai Bayon Finally Looks More Like a Box and Less Like an Egg\nDenza Launching \"God's Eye\" Driving Assistance in Europe, the Z9GT Will Get It First\n8 photos\nPhoto: Denza\nThe BYD-controlled Denza brand used the Mondial de l'Auto in Paris to announce \"God's Eye\" for Europe. Ominously named by certain accounts, the advanced driver assistance suite will be deployed in the Old Continent next year, starting with the flagship Z9GT shooting brake. City Navigation on Driving Assistance is what Denza calls the urban-centric level of God's Eye, and its functionalities will become available through an OTA update.\nCNOA was programmed and tested in China to handle point-to-point navigation through city traffic, namely the environment where ordinary assistance systems fold up. The Z9GT for Europe will soon be able to stop at red lights on its own, yield at roundabout entries, obey posted speed limits automatically, and turn left across moving traffic at busy junctions.\nCity Navigation on Driving Assistance further times its way into and out of roundabouts through gaps in the flow, a maneuver that many beginner drivers obviously dread. The folks at Denza further boast lane changes around double-parked cars and slow traffic, cyclist and pedestrian detection while driving on narrow streets, and giving way to them.\nLooking at the bigger picture, BYD is pitching Europe through Denza an easier commute. This system also comes with strings attached, the biggest one being that the driver must keep their eyes on the road to intervene whenever necessary. Beyond the software-only side of the system, the hardware is already there.\nIn the Z9GT , customers get 12 ultrasonic sensors, 11 cameras, three millimeter-wave radars, and one lidar setup. That's a whole lot of hardware, indeed, but having this many sensors and technologies working together seamlessly is the secret to preventing very problematic situations. Per UN regulation R171, systems in this class are allowed to support the driver with steering, acceleration, and braking as long as they're supervised.\nPhoto: Denza BYD and the Denza division have some coding to do before the urban-centric part of God's Eye becomes legal in the European Union. As of this writing, the software is claimed to be in an advanced stage of calibration and adaptation before seeking the R171-defined type approval required for Euro introduction. Driver's eye gaze and head posture monitoring have been confirmed, along with the position of their hands on the steering wheel.\nDenza and the BYD group as a whole line up behind Tesla at this door. Having recently renamed the incredibly misleading Full Self-Driving suite to Tesla Assisted Driving in Europe, the American system has been cleared in a bunch of countries by the Dutch approval authority RDW. A UN working-party vote on Tesla Assisted Drive is due in December 2026 after a Belgian safety group published a damning report about Full Self-Driving on October 6.\nGiven how slow this gate can swing even for Tesla, the Denza brand's 2027 target should be viewed more as a plan rather than a promise. Chinese buyers, on the other hand, use God's Eye since 2025 in the urban jungle and on highways as well. The system's Euro-specific urban capability, therefore, is roughly two years behind China due to regulatory hurdles.\nAll in all, the Z9GT is the correct spearhead for such an ambitious ADAS. The grand tourer, which is available in purely electric and Super Hybrid DM-i flavors, is a tech-leaning luxury and sporty vehicle with a lot of muscle on tap. The electric version, for example, packs 850 kilowatts (1,140 mechanical horsepower). Opting for the 2.0-liter hybrid gets you 570 kW (764 hp) at full chatter.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEurope denza z9gt denza BYD softwawre 2026 Paris Motor Show EV Paris Motor Show\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT One-Off Pagani Utopia Is a Striking Work of Art With 24K Gold and Actual Moon Dust",
